    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0311" n="299" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg299"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Potsdam/6–645: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Harry L. Hopkins,
                        Adviser to President Truman, to the
                        President</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]<anchor n="48" xml:id="d221-f1"/>
                    </seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-05-26">May 26, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>262101. Tonight Harriman and I saw Stalin and Molotov.<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d221-f2"/> Stalin told us that he had cabled you relative to the
                    pending meeting.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d221-f3"/> He clearly indicated that he
                    was anxious to meet you.</p>
                <p>We outlined the gravity of the feeling in America at great length and expressed
                    as forcibly as we could the viewpoint that you wished us to convey. The
                    importance of the Polish business was put on the line specifically. Stalin
                    listened with the utmost attention to our description of the present state of
                    American public opinion and gave us the impression that the drift of events
                    disturbed him also.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d221-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d221-f1" n="48" xml:id="d221-f1-content">
                <p>For the portion of this telegram here omitted, dealing with the establishment of
                    the Allied Control Council for Germany, see <ref target="frus1945v03#pg_309"
                        >vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p. 309</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f2" n="49" xml:id="d221-f2-content">
                <p>Foreign Commissar Molotov left the San Francisco Conference on May 8 and returned
                    to Moscow. Regarding his departure, see <ref target="frus1945v01#pg_650">vol.
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 650</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1945v01#pg_652">652</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f3" n="50" xml:id="d221-f3-content">
                <p>i.e., the Potsdam Conference. In a letter to Ambassador Harriman dated May 26
                        (<ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_85">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Conference of Berlin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Potsdam</hi>), vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 85</ref>),
                    Molotov explained that the message referred to was not from Marshal Stalin to
                    President Truman but from Molotov to Mr. Joseph E. Davies, Special
                    Representative of President Truman. For documentation regarding the physical
                    arrangements for the Potsdam Conference, see <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_85">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>., pp. 85</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d221-f4" n="51" xml:id="d221-f4-content">
                <p>For the record of this conversation held at the Kremlin, May 26, at 8 p.m., see
                    memorandum by Charles E. Bohlen, May 26, 1945, <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_24">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>., p. 24</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
